_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -15 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 12 & -7 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+4(0)-4(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” and “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” are true, while statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b86528-ce28-5eed-81ec-78f65b8bfbfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241206} {:answer/id #uuid "13ca3a73-8819-53c0-a9f2-1754b051aa7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241348} {:answer/id #uuid "8afc8c64-0071-577e-997a-b420375496e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242408} {:answer/id #uuid "a188281a-aa1c-564a-8590-9ffe987dda5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242593} {:answer/id #uuid "f49cafc4-2fa2-5c0d-b2b3-b0ffd52a4f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13ca3a73-8819-53c0-a9f2-1754b051aa7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241348} :answer-field/id #uuid "a34f1d18-acf0-53c2-9bad-5822f71a987c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "14620d69-e2fb-5946-a678-95eb17b3c5ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-107018" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1+t-2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t-2{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1+t-2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-2)+{x}_{3}(t-2{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3})t+(-2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-{x}_{3}}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e0b9263-b256-50e0-a4a6-ba633bc35b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241732} {:answer/id #uuid "52719de2-c67f-5672-93e9-f15122419795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241913} {:answer/id #uuid "74aac2e7-ea4e-53ea-b81d-c8b0735948e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242219} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18a56a-d255-5891-bd7c-e41a6e8de014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242441} {:answer/id #uuid "b574f2cd-f8f1-52cf-a086-1a35ebc969b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e0b9263-b256-50e0-a4a6-ba633bc35b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33a2ab3-338d-5a01-8ab1-6a7e6aa2dd40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "95efe695-ba47-5a50-958d-ff18cf47e088" :question/math-academy-id "q-107020" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-2+2t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-4{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-2+3t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2+2t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-4{t}^{2})+{x}_{3}(-2+3t-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3})+(2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3})t+(2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{3})-2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})}{{x}_{3}}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65d4fa9e-c9d3-5997-88e6-2959ccdec549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242097} {:answer/id #uuid "71a8d6aa-ba41-514d-9ef0-6def746b8475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242190} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cb093e-7769-57cd-9234-8bc3645a9c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242588} {:answer/id #uuid "c2bde5dd-6dcd-533f-9a1c-5e2c6e7bc7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242889} {:answer/id #uuid "f2507c67-3eab-57ee-ad9c-85efbef966e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0cb093e-7769-57cd-9234-8bc3645a9c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242588} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e05056-3b15-5bbd-9784-b99183f69e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e7aeb08-f657-5f4a-97af-db8e7705ef52" :question/math-academy-id "q-107021" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1+t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-2+3t+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{3}(t)=3t+3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}\\cdot {p}_{3}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),$ and ${p}_{3}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1+t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-2+3t+{t}^{2})+{x}_{3}(3t+3{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3})t+(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{{x}_{3}}{(-\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3})}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26908c88-4546-5c39-8a0d-31d5ea044633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241511} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea19c2f-a13c-5062-b135-dce37ac58281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242293} {:answer/id #uuid "893dfc03-9c7e-598a-9974-7948df60bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242388} {:answer/id #uuid "9a40e0a6-8ba3-5e19-a708-fa12bb1f7410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242527} {:answer/id #uuid "c64792d2-d8d6-53bc-9169-b3af2bc883ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The set is linearly independent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "893dfc03-9c7e-598a-9974-7948df60bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242388} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e7f2f3-c8c1-5726-ad04-1013e80287b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bdbf8b9-5224-5509-b2d6-bdf2f04fc1ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-107022" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3+9t.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3t)+{x}_{2}(-3+9t) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-3{x}_{2})+(-3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2})t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot column is the $1$st one. Hence, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the $1$st equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{2}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}]}
